NAME
    export - give shell variables to the programs the shell runs

SYNOPSIS
    export [-p] [NAME[=VALUE]...]...

DESCRIPTION
    Marks each NAME as exported, so that its value is placed in the
    environment of every program this shell starts. With NAME=VALUE
    the variable is given that value first; a bare NAME is created
    empty if it does not exist yet. A variable that is not exported
    belongs to the shell alone and no program ever sees it, which is
    why `FOO=1' and `export FOO=1' are not the same command.

    With no operand, and with -p, writes the exported variables in a
    form that can be read back in:

        export NAME="VALUE"

    An array cannot be exported. The environment holds NAME=VALUE
    strings and has nowhere to put a subscript, so `export a[2]' is
    refused rather than quietly exporting `a' and leaving the caller
    to wonder what happened to the element.

    Exporting says nothing about where commands are looked up: that
    is the shell's own search path, which `type' reports (see type).
    Unknown options are refused, whether or not -p was given first.
    Shell builtin.
